--  The lock threads share state behind, and the process's one record of
--  whether a thread has been started (D501, D502).  It is a module of its
--  own, beneath hosted/thread, so that the heap can take a lock without
--  importing the thread facility that allocates its own stacks from the
--  caller: hosted/heap imports this module, hosted/thread imports this
--  module, and hosted/thread never imports the heap.
--
--  A lock is one u32 cell of core/atomic: free, taken, or taken with a
--  thread waiting.  Taking a free lock and giving one nobody waits for are
--  one read-modify-write each; a thread that finds it taken sleeps in the
--  system's wait on the cell's address, platform/posix's `wait_on`, and
--  the thread that gives it wakes one waiter.  This is the three-state
--  lock of Drepper's "Futexes Are Tricky", on every hosted system alike.
--
--  On Cortex-M0 there is no lock: a context there excludes the interrupt
--  handlers that share its state with platform/cortex_m's critical
--  section, `disable_interrupts` to `restore_interrupts`, and that is its
--  lock (D501).  This module is hosted only.

import core/atomic
import platform/posix

--  What a lock's cell holds.
free: u32 = 0
taken: u32 = 1
contended: u32 = 2

lock_value: type = struct
    word: atomic.cell(u32)
end lock_value

--- A lock: one thread at a time holds it, between `take` and `give`.  A
--- zeroed lock is free, so module storage starts one `zeroed` and `new`
--- makes one for a local or a field.  It is not reentrant: a thread that
--- takes a lock it holds waits for itself forever.  It keeps no owner, so
--- nothing stops a thread giving a lock another holds; do not.  Waiting
--- blocks the thread, so a fibre that holds a lock across a switch can
--- hold every other fibre of its round that wants it.  Share it by
--- pointer, never by copy.
public lock: type = lock_value

--- A free lock.
public new: () -> (made: lock) =
    made = (word: atomic.new(free))
end new

--- Take `held`, waiting while another thread holds it.  What the thread
--- that gave it last did before giving it is visible after this returns.
public take: (held: ptr mut lock) -> none =
    word: ptr mut atomic.cell(u32) = addr held.val.word
    mut seen: u32 = atomic.compare_exchange(word, free, taken)
    return when seen == free
    --  Say that someone waits, then sleep while that is still so: whoever
    --  gives the lock sees `contended` and wakes a sleeper, and the one it
    --  wakes takes the lock as contended, since it cannot know it was the
    --  last.
    if seen <> contended then
        seen = atomic.exchange(word, contended)
    end if
    while seen <> free do
        posix.wait_on(word, contended)
        seen = atomic.exchange(word, contended)
    end while
end take

--- Take `held` if no thread holds it, and say whether it did, without
--- waiting.
public take_if_free: (held: ptr mut lock) -> (took: bool) =
    took = atomic.compare_exchange(addr held.val.word, free, taken) == free
end take_if_free

--- Give `held`, which this thread took, back, and wake one thread waiting
--- for it, if any is.  What this thread did while it held the lock is
--- visible to the thread that takes it next.
public give: (held: ptr mut lock) -> none =
    word: ptr mut atomic.cell(u32) = addr held.val.word
    if atomic.exchange(word, free) == contended then
        posix.wake_one(word)
    end if
end give

--  Whether the program has started a thread: zero until `note_start`, and
--  one for the rest of the process.  Relaxed, since the thread's creation
--  orders what the program did before it, and nothing is ever unwritten.
mut started: atomic.cell(u32) = zeroed

--- Record that the program is about to start a thread.  The thread
--- facility calls it before it starts its first thread, on the thread that
--- starts it, and the creation's happens-before edge gives the new thread
--- the record.  State that is shared only once there are threads, the
--- heap's among it, then takes its lock; until then it skips it.
public note_start: () -> none =
    atomic.store_relaxed(addr started, 1)
end note_start

--- Whether `note_start` has been called: whether a thread other than the
--- one the program started on may be running.  A C thread calling into
--- Landin under `--link=libc` is none of the program's and is not
--- counted.
public threads_started: () -> (yes: bool) =
    yes = atomic.load_relaxed(addr started) <> 0
end threads_started
